          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have stage 3 kidney disease and high blood pressure. Does the classification link the two, and what target does guidance describe for someone like me?",
          "answer": "**Does the classification link the two?** Yes. In the coding guidance (*Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family*), hypertension with chronic kidney disease is classed together as one combination code, I12, rather than as hypertension alone with kidney disease recorded separately. A second code then gives the stage of kidney disease. If heart disease is also documented, the combined code is I13. The retrieved text does not spell out a general presumption of this link for this pairing. It describes that convention for diabetes and kidney disease. Coders and clinicians make the final choice, so ask your clinician or records team if the coding on your records matters to you.\n\n**What blood pressure target applies to you?** The guidance I could retrieve does not give a target for stage 3 kidney disease with high blood pressure, so I can't give you a number that applies to you. The hypertension guidance from a major cardiology society says that when chronic kidney disease is present, drug treatment is started alongside lifestyle changes rather than after lifestyle changes alone. It also says renin-angiotensin blockade is preferred in that situation. It does not name a dose, and nothing here is an instruction to start or change any medicine.\n\n**Where the guidance differs:** The coronary artery disease guidance from the same society gives a target below 130/80 mm Hg for most people with that condition. Those passages do not say whether it applies to people with kidney disease, and it should not be taken as your target. Authorities also disagree on where high blood pressure starts. US cardiology guidance treats 130/80 mm Hg as the start of stage 1 hypertension. European guidance starts office hypertension at 140/90 mm Hg and treats 130 to 139 / 80 to 89 as high-normal. The same reading can therefore be labelled differently depending on which guidance is used.\n\n**A reasonable next step:** Ask your kidney doctor or primary care clinician which blood pressure target applies to you and which guideline they follow. Your target may depend on factors such as your urine protein levels, your age and your other conditions, and your clinician is the right person to set it.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
